From 05dcead8dc838cce2f403fca447791a1676faea3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Deus Rex <research@deusrex.ai>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 13:35:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Bug fix: c-icap: double free and use-after-free in the ICAP
 entity-list teardown Author: Deus Rex <research@deusrex.ai>

process_encapsulated() parses the Encapsulated: header and fills the fixed array
req->entities[5]. The loop guard tests "num > 5" before the write, so it accepts
num = 5 and writes req->entities[5], one element past the array. The array is
immediately followed by req->trash_entities[7] of the same type, so on the
builds we tested that write lands on trash_entities[0].

The write itself stays inside the ci_request_t allocation. The damage is what
it destroys: req->entities[5] is the slot the teardown walk in ci_request_destroy()
treats as the NULL terminator. With it populated, the first teardown loop frees one
element too far and frees trash_entities[0] as if it were entities[5], without clearing
the slot. The second loop then frees trash_entities[0] again. The second free is a
use-after-free read of the freed object followed by a double free.

An ICAP request that lists six entities plants the aliased pointer; the double free
happens later, when the worker destroys that request object. No authentication and
no user interaction are required: the header loop runs right after the request header
is read, before any service module or ACL decision.
---
 request_common.c | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/request_common.c b/request_common.c
index 75ad3c6..64cdfe4 100644
--- a/request_common.c
+++ b/request_common.c
@@ -395,7 +395,7 @@ void ci_request_reset(ci_request_t * req)
     ci_clock_time_reset(&req->stop_w_t);
     req->processing_time = 0;
 
-    for (i = 0; req->entities[i] != NULL; i++) {
+    for (i = 0; i < 5 && req->entities[i] != NULL; i++) {
         ci_request_release_entity(req, i);
     }
 
@@ -423,8 +423,10 @@ void ci_request_destroy(ci_request_t * req)
         ci_headers_destroy(req->xtrailers);
     if (req->trailer_names)
         ci_buffer_free(req->trailer_names);
-    for (i = 0; req->entities[i] != NULL; i++)
+    for (i = 0; i < 5 && req->entities[i] != NULL; i++) {
         destroy_encaps_entity(req->entities[i]);
+        req->entities[i] = NULL;
+    }
 
     for (i = 0; i < 7; i++) {
         if (req->trash_entities[i])
@@ -514,7 +516,7 @@ int process_encapsulated(ci_request_t * req, const char *buf)
         type = get_encaps_type(pos, &val, &end);
         if (type < 0) /*parse error - return "400 bad request"*/
             return EC_400;
-        if (num > 5)          /*In practice there is an error here .... */
+        if (num >= 4)          /* entities[] has 5 slots; keep index 4 as the NULL terminator the teardown walk relies on */
             break;
         if (type == ICAP_NULL_BODY)
             hasbody = 0;     /*We have not a body */
